# $XConsortium: system.twmrc,v 1.8 91/04/23 21:10:58 gildea Exp $
#
# Default twm configuration file; needs to be kept small to conserve string
# space in systems whose compilers don't handle medium-sized strings.
#
# Sites should tailor this file, providing any extra title buttons, menus, etc.
# that may be appropriate for their environment.  For example, if most of the
# users were accustomed to uwm, the defaults could be set up not to decorate
# any windows and to use meta-keys.
#
# LikeOS changes: Copyright (C) 2026 The LikeOS Project
#

#
# Fonts: Selawik, the desktop's interface font, at 9 points; window titles in
# its semibold weight.  The "xft:" prefix makes ctwm draw it anti-aliased
# through Xft -- a LikeOS addition (ports/xorg/patches/ctwm, 0003); the part
# after it is a fontconfig pattern.
#
# Selawik covers Latin only.  A character it has no glyph for -- a Greek or
# Cyrillic window title, say -- is drawn from the next font in fontconfig's
# order for the pattern that has one (patch 0004).  Noto Sans is named second
# so that this is a humanist sans that sits well beside Selawik, rather than
# whatever the generic sans-serif happens to be; scripts Noto Sans lacks fall
# through to the fonts after it (DejaVu Sans and the rest).
#
TitleFont	"xft:Selawik,Noto Sans:size=9:weight=semibold"
ResizeFont	"xft:Selawik,Noto Sans:size=9"
MenuFont	"xft:Selawik,Noto Sans:size=9"
IconFont	"xft:Selawik,Noto Sans:size=9"
IconManagerFont	"xft:Selawik,Noto Sans:size=9"
# ShowWorkSpaceManager is deliberately absent.  Workspaces are switched from
# the Workspaces button in taskbar(1) (or Super+Tab), which drives ctwm
# through _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP; ctwm still owns the workspaces themselves (see
# the WorkSpaces block below, which is what makes there be four of them and
# what ctwm advertises as _NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS).  Without this keyword the
# manager window is created but never mapped.
#
# The LikeOS window frame (ModernTitles, a LikeOS addition -- see
# ports/xorg/patches/ctwm/0003 and 0007).  A flat title bar 36 pixels tall:
# the application's icon 12 pixels in and its title after it; on the right
# minimise, maximise and close as rounded rectangles 36 x 26 pixels inset in
# the bar, TitlePadding (4) apart and FramePadding (6) from the right edge,
# each with a thin line glyph.  At rest a button is only its glyph; under the
# pointer it fills with a faint blue-grey wash, close with coral red and a
# white glyph.  The bar is #eef2f9 with near-black text when the window has
# the focus and #f5f7fb with slate text when it does not; those colours are
# built into the mode, so TitleBackground and TitleForeground below do not
# change them.
#
# Everything three-dimensional is off: the frame draws no bevels anywhere.
# The window border is one flat pixel (BorderWidth, colours in the Color
# block).  NoTitleHighlight drops the striped "this window has the focus"
# band classic ctwm draws in the title; the colour change says it instead.
#
# The rest of the frame comes with the same keyword:
#   - corners rounded to 10 pixels, the border following the curve; square
#     while a window is maximised, snapped or fullscreen.  A maximised window
#     shows no border at all: its title bar reaches the top of the screen.
#   - an invisible band a few pixels outside every resizable window that
#     resizes it, the cut-off corners included.
#   - caption buttons that act when the mouse button is let go over them, and
#     show it pressed until then, so a press can still be taken back.
#   - snapping: a window dragged by its title to the top edge of the screen
#     is maximised, to the left or right edge it fills that half of the work
#     area; a thin rounded blue outline shows where it will go while the
#     pointer is there.  Dragging a maximised or snapped window restores it
#     under the pointer.  Double-clicking a title maximises or restores.
#   - _NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED, so GTK programs know which window has the focus
#     and draw the others in the theme's backdrop state.
#   - ctwm's own menus (Alt+F1, Alt+F2) get 30-pixel rows, a rounded
#     highlight under the pointer and hairline separators.
#
# A program may draw its own title row (LikeOS Explorer puts its tabs there):
# it asks for a border only through _MOTIF_WM_HINTS (GDK's
# gdk_window_set_decorations (GDK_DECOR_BORDER)), and ctwm then gives it the
# border, the rounded corners and the resize band but no title bar.  It moves
# and resizes itself with _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE (gtk_window_begin_move_drag and
# begin_resize_drag; the move snaps like a title drag does) and maximises
# and minimises through _NET_WM_STATE and WM_CHANGE_STATE as usual.  Such a
# row should match the numbers here: 36 pixels tall, buttons 36 x 26 with
# 6-pixel corners, 4 apart, 6 from the right edge, vertically centred.
#
ModernTitles
TitleBarHeight		36
TitleButtonWidth	36
TitleButtonBorderWidth	0
FramePadding		6
TitlePadding		4
ButtonIndent		0
BorderWidth		1
NoTitleHighlight
TitleJustification	"left"
#
#
# Focus and stacking: pressing a mouse button ANYWHERE in a window focuses it
# (so its title bar highlights) and raises it.
#
# Without ClickToFocus, ctwm follows the pointer, so moving onto a second xterm
# focused it silently and pressing a button changed nothing visible.
#
# The click is NOT eaten.  This is handled in one place in HandleButtonPress,
# which sets the focus and then calls XAllowEvents(ReplayPointer) -- so the
# press still reaches the application and a click that also selects text or
# presses a button behaves normally.
#
# RaiseOnClick is set, and is safe, because the desktop is pinned to the bottom
# plane by an explicit rule -- see the OnTopPriority block further down.
#
# It had to be removed for a while: ctwm treats PCManFM's full-screen desktop as
# an ordinary window, and a window in the normal plane comes to the front when
# clicked, so clicking the background put the desktop over the whole session.
# Pinning it to -8 removes that, and clicking a window body to raise it -- which
# is the point of RaiseOnClick -- works again.
ClickToFocus
RaiseOnClick

#
# Minimise into the taskbar instead of onto the desktop.
#
# Iconifying normally leaves a little icon window lying on the root.  Unmapping
# instead means the window simply disappears, and the entry it already has in
# the icon manager at the top IS the minimised state -- click that entry to get
# the window back.
IconifyByUnmapping

#
# Title buttons: minimise, maximise, close -- in that order, left to right.
#
# NoDefaults is required.  Without it ctwm adds its own two ("dot" to iconify on
# the LEFT, "resize" on the right) and there is no way to remove just those.  It
# also drops the six fallback mouse bindings AddDefaultFuncButtons() installs
# (buttons 1 and 2 on title, icon and iconmgr) -- every one of which this file
# already binds explicitly further down, which is what makes it safe here.
#
# Right-side buttons are laid out left to right in the order declared
# (win_decorations.c walks the list adding boxwidth each time), so close ends up
# in the corner rather than where minimise is expected.
NoDefaults
RightTitleButton ":xpm:bar"     = f.iconify
RightTitleButton ":xpm:zoom"    = f.fullzoom
RightTitleButton ":xpm:cross"   = f.delete

#
# Four workspaces, switched from the Workspaces flyout in taskbar(1)
# (Super+Tab), which shows one card per workspace.  ctwm still owns the
# workspaces themselves and publishes the count as _NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS,
# which is where the panel reads it -- change the number here and the flyout
# follows.
#
# Fields per workspace, as ctwm's workspace_config.c documents them:
#     "name" { button-bg  button-fg  map-bg  map-fg  [root-image] }
#
# Field 3 is the background of each workspace's CELL in the workspace manager --
# the grey the little window blocks are drawn on.
#
# ctwm normally uses that same colour for the DESKTOP too
# (XSetWindowBackground(vs->window, ws->backcp.back) in workspace_manager.c), so
# grey cells would have forced a grey desktop.  DontPaintRootWindow, set below,
# switches only the root half of that off: the cells still take field 3, and the
# desktop is left to the xsetroot in xinitrc.  The two are independent now, which
# is what lets the cells be grey and the desktop teal.
#
# The root-image field upstream used is dropped: one entry named an .xpm that is
# not installed here, and the other asked for the "plaid" pattern -- both only
# per-workspace, so the background changed as you switched desktops.
#
WorkSpaces {
    "1" {"#c6c6c6" "black" "#c6c6c6" "black"}
    "2" {"#c6c6c6" "black" "#c6c6c6" "black"}
    "3" {"#c6c6c6" "black" "#c6c6c6" "black"}
    "4" {"#c6c6c6" "black" "#c6c6c6" "black"}
}

#
# Mouse pointers.
#
# ctwm's defaults are the old X set: "top_left_arrow" over frames, titles and
# icons (the thin skewed outline arrow), "hand2" over the title buttons and
# "sb_left_arrow" over menus.  "left_ptr" is the plain arrow every current
# desktop uses, and it is what these should all be.
#
# Kept as they are, because they say something a plain arrow could not:
#   Move/Resize  fleur  - a drag is in progress
#   Wait         watch  - busy
#   Destroy      pirate - the next click KILLS the window it lands on; that is
#                         worth a pointer that does not look routine
#
# The DESKTOP pointer is not here: ctwm never sets a cursor on the root window,
# so the X-shaped one comes from the server's own default and has to be replaced
# with xsetroot -cursor_name (see xinitrc).
#
Cursors
{
    Frame    "left_ptr"
    Title    "left_ptr"
    Icon     "left_ptr"
    IconMgr  "left_ptr"
    Button   "left_ptr"
    Menu     "left_ptr"
    Select   "left_ptr"
    Move     "fleur"
    Resize   "fleur"
    Wait     "watch"
    Destroy  "pirate"
}

###########################################################
# Desktop bar (bottom of the screen)
###########################################################

#
# No workspace manager geometry.
#
# ctwm's workspace manager is never mapped (ShowWorkSpaceManager is absent at
# the top of this file), so a geometry for it would describe a window nobody
# sees.  Workspaces are switched from the Workspaces flyout in taskbar(1)
# (Super+Tab), which sends _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP; the WorkSpaces block above
# still defines the workspaces themselves and is what ctwm reports as
# _NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS, which is where the taskbar learns there are four.

# Leave the root window alone.  Without this ctwm repaints the desktop from the
# current workspace's field 3 every time a workspace is mapped, which would tie
# the desktop colour to the workspace cell colour.
DontPaintRootWindow

#
# No icon manager.
#
# ctwm's icon manager WAS the taskbar: a grid of one cell per window across the
# top of the screen.  taskbar(1) replaces it, at the bottom of the screen -- a
# GTK program that reads _NET_CLIENT_LIST and drives windows with the standard
# client messages, so it needs nothing from ctwm but the EWMH properties ctwm
# already publishes.
#
# NoIconManagers rather than IconManagerDontShow: the latter only hides entries,
# leaving the manager window itself created, mapped and taking a strip of the
# screen.  This stops it being built at all.
NoIconManagers

#
# Clients that are the panel rather than applications: undecorated, on every
# workspace, and never given a task button.
#
# taskbar(1) sets _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DOCK, which ctwm understands and stacks
# accordingly, and _NET_WM_STATE_SKIP_TASKBAR, which the panel itself honours.
# These lists are belt and braces for the two standalone widgets, which are no
# longer started by xinitrc but can still be run by hand.
#
# AlwaysOnTop is deliberately NOT set.  It works -- but it also wins against
# every window that overlaps the bar, and a new xterm placed under it had its
# title bar hidden, leaving nothing to grab to move the window.  With normal
# stacking new windows come up ABOVE the bar.
#
NoTitle
{
    "taskbar"
    "datetime"
    "load"
}

NoBorder
{
    "taskbar"
    "datetime"
    "load"
}

OccupyAll
{
    "taskbar"
    "datetime"
    "load"
}

#
# The LikeOS light palette.
#
# The window border is one pixel of cool grey-blue, a shade darker on the
# window that has the focus: ctwm paints the FOCUSED window's border in
# BorderColor and every other one from the BorderTile pair (the same colour
# twice, so the tile is solid).  There is no compositor here to draw shadows,
# so the darker edge is what tells the focused window from the ones behind
# it.  The rounded corners draw their curves in the same two colours.
#
# Menus are white with dark text and a pale blue rounded highlight under the
# pointer (the "fore":"back" pair after each menu name below sets that).
# Menu titles are quiet slate text rather than a coloured band.  With
# ModernTitles the menu's border is drawn in MenuShadowColor, a light
# blue-grey hairline, and the separators in a lighter shade of it (the drop
# shadow MenuShadowColor is otherwise for is switched off, see NoMenuShadows).
#
# FrameBackground is what a window shows where its application has not drawn
# yet -- the strip a resize uncovers while the program is busy, or a window
# just taken over when the window manager restarts after a resolution change.
# White, like the application's own background, rather than the border tile.
#
Color
{
    BorderColor           "#aebad0"
    DefaultBackground     "#f5f7fb"
    DefaultForeground     "#141a24"
    BorderTileBackground  "#d3d9e3"
    BorderTileForeground  "#d3d9e3"
    FrameBackground       "#ffffff"
    TitleBackground       "#f5f7fb"
    TitleForeground       "#141a24"
    MenuBackground        "#ffffff"
    MenuForeground        "#141a24"
    MenuTitleBackground   "#ffffff"
    MenuTitleForeground   "#586174"
    MenuShadowColor       "#d3dae6"
    IconBackground        "#f5f7fb"
    IconForeground        "#141a24"
    IconBorderColor       "#d3d9e3"
    IconManagerBackground "#f5f7fb"
    IconManagerForeground "#141a24"
    IconManagerHighlight  "#dce7fd"
    MapWindowBackground   "#2a5bd7"
    MapWindowForeground   "white"
}

# The IconManagerGeometry, IconManagerDontShow and NoTitle that upstream set
# here are gone: there is no icon manager any more (NoIconManagers above), and
# the panel's own window lists live in the "Top desktop bar" section.  Removed
# rather than left in place: ctwm accepts several of these without complaint --
# the window lists would simply accumulate and the last geometry would silently
# win -- so a leftover copy here would be a trap for whoever next changes the
# bar and cannot see why it has no effect.
#
# Define some useful functions for motion-based actions.
#
MoveDelta 3
Function "move-or-lower" { f.move f.deltastop f.lower }
Function "move-or-raise" { f.move f.deltastop f.raise }
Function "move-or-iconify" { f.move f.deltastop f.iconify }

#
# Set some useful bindings.  Sort of uwm-ish, sort of simple-button-ish
#
#
# Root menus: Alt+F1 and Alt+F2 from the keyboard, and the Button1/Button2
# root bindings below for when the desktop is not running.
#
# The mouse route over the background is deliberately NOT wired up while the
# desktop runs, and the reason is worth writing down because it looks like
# something that ought to work.  A window manager only hears about root-window
# clicks by selecting ButtonPressMask ON the root -- it does not grab them --
# and PCManFM's desktop covers the root completely, so a click on the
# background stops at PCManFM and the bindings below never fire.  PCManFM can
# be patched to hand the click back (that is what show_wm_menu does for button
# 3), and that patch existed here for button 1.  It was removed.
#
# What it cost: with StayUpMenus, a press that does not select an entry leaves
# the menu POSTED, and ctwm returns from HandleButtonRelease before it reaches
# its own XUngrabPointer -- so it keeps the pointer grab it took to post the
# menu.  Every click after that goes to ctwm rather than to the window under
# the cursor, and the first one is spent dismissing the menu.  Clicking the
# desktop and then clicking a file-manager window did nothing until the window
# was clicked a second time.  That is ctwm behaving as designed for a stay-up
# menu; it is only intolerable because button 1 on the desktop is a button
# people press without meaning to open anything.
#
# So button 1 on the blank desktop is PCManFM's again, which is upstream's own
# behaviour: rubber-band selection.  Button 3 stays PCManFM's as well
# (show_wm_menu=0), so the desktop keeps its own context menu.
#
# StayUpMenus is what makes a posted menu stay: without it a ctwm menu is
# press-drag-release, so it would vanish the instant the button came up.  With
# it, a press that does not select simply leaves the menu posted, and the next
# click either activates the entry under the pointer or dismisses it.  That is
# what you want from Alt+F1, where the menu is asked for deliberately.
#
StayUpMenus

#
# No drop shadow under menus.  ctwm's is a solid black copy of the menu
# offset by a few pixels, which reads as a hard dark slab beside a light menu;
# a soft shadow would need a compositor.  The hairline border stands in.
NoMenuShadows

#
# PCManFM's desktop, named explicitly.
#
# ctwm is built with EWMH and does read _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE (EwmhGetProperties,
# add_window.c), and PCManFM does set the DESKTOP type hint in
# fm_desktop_constructor -- but f.identify reports `OnTopPriority = 0' for that
# window, which is the NORMAL plane.  A window ctwm had typed as a desktop
# would report -8, since EwmhGetInitPriority hands it EWMH_PRI_DESKTOP.  Every
# link in that chain checks out on its own and the result still says the type
# never arrives, so this states it outright rather than relying on it.
#
# Keyed on WM_NAME, which the port patch
# 0003-name-the-desktop-window-so-a-wm-rule-can-match-it gives the window.  It
# has to be the name: ctwm's LookInList matches WM_NAME, then res_name, then
# res_class, and res_name/res_class are "pcmanfm" for the desktop AND for every
# file-manager window, so a rule keyed on either would pin the file manager to
# the bottom too.
#
# -8 is the same plane EWMH_PRI_DESKTOP would have given it: below everything,
# so no click can lift it over the session.  NoTitle and NoBorder match what
# EwmhHasTitle/EwmhHasBorder return for a desktop-typed window.
#
OnTopPriority -8
{
    "pcmanfm-desktop"
}

NoTitle
{
    "pcmanfm-desktop"
}

NoBorder
{
    "pcmanfm-desktop"
}


# Dead while the desktop is running -- it covers the root, so these never
# fire -- and kept for the session where it is not (a bare startx, or after
# the desktop has died), which is exactly when a way into the menus matters.
Button1 =	: root		: f.menu "defops"
Button2 =	: root		: f.menu "windowops"

# The route that always works, desktop or no desktop.
"F1" = m	: all		: f.menu "defops"
"F2" = m	: all		: f.menu "windowops"

Button1 = m	: window|icon	: f.function "move-or-lower"
Button2 = m	: window|icon	: f.iconify
Button3 = m	: window|icon	: f.function "move-or-raise"

Button1 =	: title		: f.function "move-or-raise"
Button2 =	: title		: f.raiselower
Button1 = m     : title		: f.movetitlebar

Button1 =	: icon		: f.function "move-or-iconify"
Button2 =	: icon		: f.iconify

Button1 =	: iconmgr	: f.iconify
Button2 =	: iconmgr	: f.iconify
# Button 3 was free here.  It raises the taskbar itself, which is the way back
# when a window has come up over the bar (see the panel section above).
Button3 =	: iconmgr	: f.raise

Button1 =	: frame		: f.resize
Button1 = m	: frame		: f.move
Button2 =	: frame		: f.raiselower
#
# And a menus with the usual things
#
#
# Root menu.  Only programs that are actually installed appear here: upstream
# offered xcalc, xman and xmag, none of which is in this port, and a menu entry
# that runs a command the system does not have is worse than no entry -- it
# fails silently, with nothing on screen to say why.
#
# When a program is added to ports/xorg/packages.list and staged, add it here.
#
# The browser is named the way res/xorg/applications/renames names it:
# program and role together, so the entry says what it opens.
#
menu "defops" ("#141a24":"#eef3fd")
{
    "LikeOS"		f.title
    "Xterm"		!"xterm&"
    "LikeOS Explorer"	!"likeos-explorer &"
    "Editor"		!"mousepad &"
    "Mail"		!"claws-mail &"
    "Luakit Web Browser"	!"luakit &"
    "LikeOS Media Player"	!"mediaplayer &"
    "Calculator"	!"galculator &"
    ""			f.separator
    "Restart session"	f.restart
    "Exit session"	f.function "exit-session"
}

#
# Leaving the desktop goes through likeos-logout, as the taskbar and the
# desktop menu do: it starts the log-out sound and sends ctwm SIGTERM, on
# which it shuts down.  (f.exec starts commands in the background, so a sound
# and an f.quit in one function would race; the helper keeps the order.)
#
Function "exit-session"
{
    f.exec "likeos-logout &"
}

#
# PCManFM's desktop is started by xinitrc, not from here.  It draws the home
# folder, the trash and any mounted volume on the background, and it owns the
# right button over that background -- which is why the root menu above is on
# the left one.  A desktop is part of the session, not something to be switched
# on from a menu after the fact.
#

menu "windowops" ("#141a24":"#eef3fd") {
    "Window"		f.title
    "Occupy"		f.occupy
    "Occupy All"	f.occupyall
    "Iconify"		f.iconify
    "Refresh"		f.refresh
    "Vanish"		f.vanish
    "Info"		f.identify
    ""			f.separator
    "Resize"		f.resize
    "Move"		f.move
    "Raise"		f.raise
    "Lower"		f.lower
    "Animate"		f.startanimation
    "Don't animate"	f.stopanimation
    ""			f.separator
    "Send Delete"	f.delete
    "Kill Window"	f.destroy
    "Pin menu"		f.pin
}
